Object.assign(window.deepArticles,{
  "hugh-gaitskell":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"Hugh Gaitskell",eyebrow:"Prime Minister of the United Kingdom · 1960–1963",infoboxKicker:"Commonwealth Labour statesman",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 -->Hugh Todd Naylor Gaitskell was born in 1906 and completed his education and early economic formation before the divergence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His succession to Aneurin Bevan in 1960, consolidation of Labour government and practical space objectives, Ariel I milestone, January 1963 death, and succession by Harold Wilson are accepted setting chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 -->Gaitskell's birth, education, teaching, and economic and civil-service formation before August 1941 are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The author directly establishes the 1957 German breakthrough as a shock that makes Britain rush a space programme from a weak position.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Gaitskell's later wartime service, 1945 Parliament entry, Labour career, 1960–1963 premiership, Commonwealth policy, practical space programme, Ariel I, death in office, and Wilson succession are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Cabinet, individual domestic measures, and constituency mechanics remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/portraits/hugh-gaitskell.jpg",alt:"Portrait of Hugh Gaitskell",caption:"Hugh Gaitskell"},
    primarySources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Gaitskell",label:"Hugh Gaitskell — historical biography"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early life and economics",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>Gaitskell was born in London on 9 April 1906, educated at Winchester and New College, Oxford, and entered teaching, economics, and wartime civil-service work before August 1941.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The later emphasis placed upon budgets, productive capacity, and institutions in his politics is compatible biographical interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"parliament",title:"Parliament and Labour",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>Gaitskell's 1945 election for Leeds South and rise as a leading Labour economic spokesman are accepted post-divergence career development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> His focus upon reconstruction, sterling scarcity, shipping, and industrial priorities after the long war and British defeat is compatible setting interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Election mechanics and early offices remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"bevan",title:"Rivalry and accommodation with Bevan",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>Gaitskell's accepted rivalry and accommodation with <a href='aneurin-bevan.html'>Aneurin Bevan</a> concern temperament, constituency, and the reach of public control.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The judgment that the fragmented Brick Parliament forces accommodation and helps make Labour a governing party of the Commonwealth settlement is compatible political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Negotiations and factional alignments remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"succession",title:"Succession in 1960",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>Bevan's illness during Labour's 1959 return, his death on 6 July 1960, and Gaitskell's succession without repudiating the preceding health, housing, shipping, or strategic institutions are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Leadership mechanics and cabinet composition remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"government",title:"Administrative government",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>Gaitskell's pursuit of parliamentary supervision of corporations, disciplined investment, social provision matched to export capacity, and negotiated Commonwealth obligations is accepted government development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The characterization of his ministry as consolidation rather than a new constitutional settlement is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Legislative instruments remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"commonwealth",title:"Labour and the Commonwealth",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>Gaitskell's connection of employment, welfare, energy, shipping, and defense to one Commonwealth material system, and Labour's resulting contest over accountability rather than rapid strategic retreat, are accepted party and government development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Speeches, votes, and policy texts remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"space",title:"Ariel I",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that Germany's 1957 space breakthrough shocks Britain into rushing its own programme from a poor starting position.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Gaitskell's practical consolidation, limited Anglo-American cooperation, and the 1962 launch of Ariel I as Britain's first satellite are accepted programme history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact budget decisions and public rhetoric remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"death",title:"Death and succession",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-g2k8k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:00:00-06:00 --><p>Gaitskell's sudden death on 18 January 1963 from complications associated with lupus and Harold Wilson's succession are accepted setting chronology, despite occurring after divergence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> His later Labour reputation as a bridge from Bevan's reconstruction to Wilson's statecraft is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"leeds.html",kicker:"Parliamentary and regional base",label:"Leeds"},{href:"aneurin-bevan.html",kicker:"Predecessor",label:"Aneurin Bevan"},{href:"harold-wilson.html",kicker:"Successor",label:"Harold Wilson"},{href:"commonwealth-labour.html",kicker:"Party tradition",label:"Commonwealth Labour"},{href:"postwar-british-governments.html",kicker:"Political sequence",label:"Postwar British Governments"}],
    facts:[["Historical birth","9 April 1906"],["Accepted death","18 January 1963"],["Accepted party","Commonwealth Labour"],["Accepted constituency","Leeds South"],["Accepted premiership","1960–1963"],["Accepted predecessor","Aneurin Bevan"],["Accepted successor","Harold Wilson"],["Accepted space milestone","Ariel I, 1962"]]
  }),

  "harold-wilson":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"Harold Wilson",eyebrow:"Prime Minister of the United Kingdom · 1963–1964 and 1969–1976",infoboxKicker:"Commonwealth Labour prime minister",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 -->James Harold Wilson was born in 1916 and completed his Oxford education before the divergence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> He is accepted as prime minister in 1963–1964 and again from 1969 to 1976.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author directly establishes that Wilson leads Britain to victory in the Kuwait conflict and that Denis Healey subsequently takes over Labour.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 -->Wilson's birth, education, and pre-August 1941 economic and civil-service formation are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His leadership in the British victory over Iraq in Kuwait and Healey's succession afterward are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His post-1941 administrative career, Parliament entry, Commonwealth Labour role, 1963–1964, 1969–1972, and 1972–1976 ministries, Accra, Powell opposition, Operation Covenant, 1975 election, and voluntary retirement are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Election dates, cabinets, and post-divergence death remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/portraits/harold-wilson.jpg",alt:"Portrait of Harold Wilson",caption:"Harold Wilson"},
    primarySources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harold_Wilson",label:"Harold Wilson — historical biography"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early life and administration",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>Wilson was born at Huddersfield on 11 March 1916, studied at Jesus College, Oxford, and entered economic and civil-service work before August 1941.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His later statistical work, fuel-policy experience, administrative planning, and belief in technical command of production are accepted post-divergence biography and interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"labour",title:"Technocratic Labour",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>Wilson's 1945 entry into Parliament, advocacy of scientific industry and regional development, and place in <a href='commonwealth-labour.html'>Commonwealth Labour</a> are accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The formula combining Crown, social provision, public corporations, strategic defense, and modernization is accepted party synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Constituency and early offices remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"first",title:"First ministry and Accra",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>Wilson's succession to Gaitskell in January 1963, Labour's <a href='ghana-independence-act.html'>Ghana Independence Act</a>, the <a href='accra-disaster.html'>Accra Disaster</a>, and Powell's 1964 victory are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author's direct premise is that a Labour government grants the failed independence and suffers a political scandal that permanently damages the party.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The ministry's precise fall and election mechanics remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"opposition",title:"Opposition to Powell",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>Wilson's opposition to permanent constitutional ceilings while accepting defense guarantees and regional consent is accepted political reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> His criticism that Powell converts the lesson of Accra into indefinite dependency is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Speeches, votes, and policy texts remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"return",title:"Return to government, 1969–1972",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>Wilson's return in 1969 through a Liberal-supported minority, negotiated budgets and strategic votes, partial softening of Ordered Particularism, and preservation of the Crown, Cape route, nuclear force, and principal public corporations are accepted government history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Agreements and legislative program remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"majority",title:"Government, 1972–1976",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>A small Labour majority from 1972, industrial modernization, selective constitutional reform, and continued planning under the 1968 Atlantic settlement are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Election date, seat totals, legislation, and the balance between domestic reform and strategic continuity remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"kuwait",title:"Kuwait conflict",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that Wilson's government wins the British conflict against Iraq in Kuwait.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1974 date, <a href='operation-covenant.html'>Operation Covenant</a>, Healey as Defence Minister, Callaghan's diplomacy, Muscat settlement, and 1975 election result are accepted military and political chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact election date and cabinet decisions remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"retirement",title:"Succession and reputation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-w2c7k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:00:00-06:00 --><p>Healey taking over Labour after Wilson's Kuwait victory is directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Wilson's voluntary retirement in 1976 after settlement and demobilization, and a succession without party crisis, are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> His reputation for recovering from Accra while preserving an unequal imperial system is compatible historical interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"hugh-gaitskell.html",kicker:"First predecessor",label:"Hugh Gaitskell"},{href:"enoch-powell.html",kicker:"Rival and intervening premier",label:"Enoch Powell"},{href:"denis-healey.html",kicker:"Successor",label:"Denis Healey"},{href:"kuwait-war.html",kicker:"Defining victory",label:"Kuwait War"}],
    facts:[["Historical birth","11 March 1916"],["Post-divergence death","Open"],["Accepted party","Commonwealth Labour"],["Accepted first ministry","1963–1964"],["Accepted minority ministry","1969–1972"],["Accepted majority ministry","1972–1976"],["Established defining war","Kuwait conflict"],["Established successor","Denis Healey"]]
  }),

  "enoch-powell":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"Enoch Powell",eyebrow:"Prime Minister of the United Kingdom · 1964–1969",infoboxKicker:"Crown-and-Commonwealth constitutionalist",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 -->John Enoch Powell was born in 1912 and became a classical scholar and professor before the divergence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> He later becomes a Crown-and-Commonwealth parliamentarian and the accepted prime minister of 1964–1969.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author assigns him a direct role as the principal Westminster voice willing to defend British African particularism.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 -->Powell's birth, early scholarship, Sydney professorship, and 1939 enlistment are inherited pre-divergence facts.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His use as the parliamentary voice of white African arguments and a modernized civilizing mission is directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His post-1941 service, offices, distinct constitutional current, Accra rise, 1964–1969 premiership, Ordered Particularism, Accra Clause, Prospero I, and Atlantic Accord consequences are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Coalition vehicle and post-divergence death remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/portraits/enoch-powell.jpg",alt:"Enoch Powell in a television interview",caption:"Enoch Powell, 1987"},
    primarySources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enoch_Powell",label:"Enoch Powell — historical biography"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early life and scholarship",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>Powell was born in Birmingham on 16 June 1912 and became professor of Greek at the University of Sydney at twenty-five.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The later importance assigned to language, history, and sovereign political communities in his constitutional thought is accepted biographical interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"war",title:"War service and India",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>Powell enlisted as a private in 1939.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His later wartime service in the Middle East and India, final brigadier rank, and use of that experience to argue for particular nations and enforceable commitments are accepted post-divergence biography.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Assignments, promotions, and final wartime post remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"parliament",title:"Crown, Parliament, and Commonwealth",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly assigns Powell a parliamentary role defending African colonists' particularist arguments and negotiating with their moral claims.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His placement in a Crown-and-Commonwealth constitutional current distinct from Mosley's corporatism, with emphasis on Crown-in-Parliament, specific treaties, and performable duties, is accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"office",title:"Crown and Commonwealth Affairs",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>Butler's appointment of Powell after the 1955 election as <a href='minister-state-crown-commonwealth-affairs.html'>Minister of State for Crown and Commonwealth Affairs</a> and parliamentary representative on the Commonwealth Council is accepted career reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Briefs, tours, Privy Council work, protected-state questions, and constitutionally mediated access to Elizabeth II are compatible administrative detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Appointment instrument and tenure remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"falsehoods",title:"Accepted Two Falsehoods",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly calls for Powell to deliver an alternate counterpart to his historical divisive speech, defending a civilizing mission whose endpoint has receded.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Two Falsehoods title, its rejection of one constitutional endpoint and unsupported abstract rights, and its connection to <a href='ordered-particularism.html'>Ordered Particularism</a> are accepted formulation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Date, text, venue, and public reception remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"accra",title:"The Accra rise",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that the failed independence appears to vindicate Powell and the white African arguments.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The <a href='accra-disaster.html'>Ghana crisis of 1963</a>, Powell's 1964 election victory, and his mandate for enforceable guarantees are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Coalition mechanism and election detail remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"government",title:"Government, 1964–1969",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>Powell's 1964–1969 premiership, codification of the Accra Clause, defended Commonwealth associations, constitutional particularism, and the 1965 independent launch of Prospero I are accepted government history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Legislative instruments, cabinet composition, and the balance between cultural severity and scientific capacity remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"atlantic",title:"Atlantic Accord and departure",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>The 1968 Atlantic Accord's division of Canada and Britain between American and European strategic spheres, its validation of Powell's sovereignty warnings, and the return of Wilson in 1969 are accepted political chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Powell's severity, inflexibility, and defense burdens as explanations for his single substantial Parliament are compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"legacy",title:"Legacy and controversy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-p4m9n writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes Powell's importance as the metropolitan voice of British African particularism.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His later influence through the Accra Clause, Crown-and-Commonwealth Conservatism, and parts of the National Liberal coalition is accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Criticism that formally non-racial qualifications reproduce racial power and permanent ceilings is compatible analysis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"minister-state-crown-commonwealth-affairs.html",kicker:"Butler-era office",label:"Crown and Commonwealth Affairs"},{href:"second-butler-ministry.html",kicker:"Government service",label:"Second Butler Ministry"},{href:"ordered-particularism.html",kicker:"Political doctrine",label:"Ordered Particularism"},{href:"british-africa.html",kicker:"Principal arena",label:"British Africa"},{href:"harold-wilson.html",kicker:"Predecessor and successor",label:"Harold Wilson"},{href:"national-liberal-movement.html",kicker:"Later inheritance",label:"National Liberal Movement"}],
    facts:[["Historical birth","16 June 1912"],["Post-divergence death","Open"],["Accepted political current","Crown and Commonwealth"],["Accepted premiership","1964–1969"],["Accepted predecessor","Harold Wilson"],["Accepted successor","Harold Wilson"],["Accepted doctrine","Ordered Particularism"],["Accepted space milestone","Prospero I, 1965"]]
  }),

  "denis-healey":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"Denis Healey",eyebrow:"Prime Minister of the United Kingdom · 1976–1980",infoboxKicker:"Commonwealth Labour prime minister",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 -->Denis Winston Healey was born in 1917, studied at Oxford, and entered wartime army service before the divergence.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The author directly establishes that Healey takes over Labour after Wilson's Kuwait victory and influences policies close enough to socialism to help make the public willing to try Thatcher's alternative.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His Defence Ministry during the Kuwait War, 1976–1980 premiership, strategic-industry programme, and eventual defeat are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 -->Healey's birth, Oxford formation, and military status before August 1941 are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His succession after Wilson's Kuwait victory and the near-socialist policy direction that prepares public acceptance of Thatcher are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His later wartime service, postwar Labour career, Defence Ministry, operational role in Kuwait, 1976–1980 premiership, strategic-public-corporation programme, clearing crisis, and defeat by the National Liberal–Constitutional Union are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact measures, later offices, and post-canon death remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/portraits/denis-healey.jpg",alt:"Portrait of Denis Healey",caption:"Denis Healey"},
    primarySources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denis_Healey",label:"Denis Healey — historical biography"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early life and war service",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>Healey was born in Mottingham on 30 August 1917, studied at Balliol College, Oxford, and entered army service before August 1941.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> His later service through North Africa, Sicily, Italy, and Anzio, eventual rank of major, and the effect of those experiences upon his concern with logistics and sustainable force are accepted post-divergence biography and interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"labour",title:"Labour strategist",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>Healey's postwar rise as a Labour defense and international-policy figure is accepted career development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> In the altered postwar order, his combination of social democracy with Commonwealth bases, nuclear deterrence, and industrial depth is compatible political interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Constituency, offices, and exact policy texts remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"defence",title:"Defence Minister",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>Healey's Defence Ministry under Wilson and his work on standardization, reserve shipping, airlift, sealift, and Commonwealth operational planning are accepted government history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The portfolio's role in demonstrating that Labour can manage imperial force without Conservative paternalism or Mosleyite command economics is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"kuwait",title:"Operation Covenant",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>Healey's service as Defence Minister during the 1974 Kuwait War and his operational political credit for reinforcement, logistics, limited objectives, and the successful campaign are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author directly establishes that Labour's control of government is why Britain does not attempt the complete conquest of Iraq.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact cabinet arguments and division of credit remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"succession",title:"Succession to Wilson",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that Healey takes over Labour after Wilson wins in Kuwait.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Wilson's 1976 retirement after settlement and demobilization, Healey's simultaneous succession as prime minister, and his promise to convert wartime competence into housing, power, technical investment, exports, and stable employment are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Leadership mechanics remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"corporations",title:"The strategic public corporations",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes a Healey policy turn close enough to socialism to trouble the public and prepare willingness to try a new alternative.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Public control of nuclear power, rail, telecommunications, dockyards, arsenals, launch capacity, and other strategic systems, together with modernization investment and treatment of skilled workforces as reserves, is the accepted form of that programme.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"crisis",title:"Clearing and contracts crisis",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>The 1978–1980 interaction of soft budgets, closure vetoes, price controls, high personal taxation, and producer interests is accepted economic development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Firms meeting plans without clearing inventories or costs, contracts outrunning reliable delivery, and strategic protection spreading into ordinary industrial shelter are compatible causal interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact fiscal measures and crisis chronology remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"defeat",title:"Defeat and legacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-h5n2k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:15:00-06:00 --><p>Thatcher's use of the crisis to argue for preserving capacity through hard accounts, ownership, and competition, followed by Healey's 1980 defeat, is accepted political chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The judgment that later Thatcherite military strength still depends upon assets, skills, and reserve facilities preserved under Healey is compatible interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Election mechanics and Healey's later offices remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"harold-wilson.html",kicker:"Predecessor",label:"Harold Wilson"},{href:"kuwait-war.html",kicker:"Defining campaign",label:"Kuwait War"},{href:"commonwealth-labour.html",kicker:"Party",label:"Commonwealth Labour"},{href:"margaret-thatcher.html",kicker:"Successor",label:"Margaret Thatcher"}],
    facts:[["Historical birth","30 August 1917"],["Post-canon death","Open"],["Accepted party","Commonwealth Labour"],["Accepted wartime rank","Major"],["Accepted cabinet role","Minister of Defence"],["Accepted premiership","1976–1980"],["Established predecessor","Harold Wilson"],["Accepted successor","Margaret Thatcher"]]
  }),

  "margaret-thatcher":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"Margaret Thatcher",eyebrow:"Prime Minister of the United Kingdom · 1980–1987",infoboxKicker:"National Liberal prime minister",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 -->Margaret Hilda Roberts was born in Grantham in 1925 and spent her pre-divergence childhood in a shopkeeping household.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> The author directly defines the setting's Thatcher as conservative in some manners but economically liberal, a shock to the established order whose reforms improve the economy in some respects without making her an infallible figure.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Her later chemistry and legal careers, National Liberal vehicle, 1980–1987 premiership, mixed-economy settlement, and succession are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 -->Thatcher's birth and formation before August 1941 are inherited history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Her liberal-economic shock, factory lease-to-buy concession, initial Falklands overconfidence, adaptation during the hard war, acceptance of responsibility and victory, and voluntary exhausted retirement after the 1986 conclusion are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Oxford chemistry, legal qualification, Conservative and National Liberal careers, NLCU leadership, 1980 victory, named 1981 law, election totals, late-1987 retirement date, and David Ashcombe succession are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Constituency, early offices, exact retirement date, and post-canon death remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    portrait:{src:"assets/portraits/margaret-thatcher.jpg",alt:"Portrait of Margaret Thatcher",caption:"Margaret Thatcher, 1984"},
    primarySources:[{href:"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Thatcher",label:"Margaret Thatcher — historical biography"}],
    sections:[
      {id:"early",title:"Early life, chemistry, and law",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>Thatcher was born Margaret Roberts in Grantham on 13 October 1925 and grew up in a shopkeeping household before August 1941.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='p'>[p]</sup> Her later chemistry studies at Somerville College, Oxford, laboratory work, legal qualification, tax practice, municipal politics, and the effect of those experiences upon her ideas of property and administration are accepted post-divergence biography and interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"conservative",title:"A Conservative beginning",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>Thatcher's beginning in Conservative politics and her friction with paternal administration, Crown-and-Commonwealth nationalism, and interests protected by the Contract Commonwealth are accepted political reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author directly establishes the underlying contrast: she appears conservative in some manners while introducing an economically liberal position without a setting precedent.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Constituency and early offices remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"movement",title:"The National Liberal turn",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>Thatcher's organization of a <a href='national-liberal-movement.html'>National Liberal movement</a> among small businesses, professionals, technical entrepreneurs, managers, homeowners, anti-corporatist Conservatives, and economically liberal Commonwealth nationalists is accepted party development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its combination of a freer internal economy with strong defense and strategic national capacity is the accepted vehicle for the author's liberal-economic premise.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"union",title:"Formation of the NLCU",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>The 1980 union of National Liberals, Liberals, defecting Conservatives, constitutional market liberals, and some Powellites in the <a href='national-liberal-constitutional-union.html'>National Liberal–Constitutional Union</a> is accepted political chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its promise to reform the economy without surrendering the Crown, Commonwealth, armed forces, or necessary industrial assets is compatible party synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Formation mechanics remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"election",title:"The 1980 election",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>The NLCU's 1980 victory with 337 seats against Commonwealth Labour's 274 and Thatcher's mandate during the clearing and contracts crisis are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author directly requires her arrival to be a shock to the established system and her economic reforms to produce real but limited improvement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Exact election date and vote totals remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"reconstitution",title:"Preserve. Lease. Transfer.",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes Thatcher's concession that failed de facto public factories pass temporarily to the state and are leased on purchase terms to new private operators, often locally rooted, so productive plant and many jobs can survive even when reopening requires months.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Industrial Reconstitution and Reownership Act of 1981, its Preserve–Lease–Transfer classification, and the retained list of public strategic assets are accepted institutional form.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"falklands",title:"Falklands War and Runway Election",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes Thatcher's imprudent initial confidence, an early failure that stains her reputation, adaptation to a long and costly televised war, and acceptance of responsibility alongside the eventual victory.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Argentina's 1984 seizure, the failed Second Torch plan, the shift to blockade and methodical recovery, and the mid-1985 Runway Election result are accepted chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Cabinet and election detail remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"retirement",title:"Victory, inquiry, and retirement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-t8r6k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:45:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes the hard-won conclusion in 1986 and an exhausted Thatcher choosing an early dissolution, declining to run again, and retiring voluntarily rather than being defeated.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Punta del Este settlement, demobilization and inquiry work, late-1987 timing, and selection of David Ashcombe as successor are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact dissolution and retirement dates remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"national-liberal-movement.html",kicker:"Political current",label:"National Liberal Movement"},{href:"national-liberal-constitutional-union.html",kicker:"Governing party",label:"National Liberal–Constitutional Union"},{href:"falklands-war.html",kicker:"Defining conflict",label:"Falklands–Malvinas War"},{href:"david-ashcombe.html",kicker:"Successor",label:"David Ashcombe"}],
    facts:[["Historical birth","13 October 1925"],["Post-canon death","Open"],["Accepted party","National Liberal–Constitutional Union"],["Accepted premiership","1980–1987"],["Accepted 1980 result","337 seats"],["Established economic device","Factory lease-to-buy"],["Established war outcome","British victory, 1986"],["Accepted successor","David Ashcombe"]]
  }),

  "commonwealth-labour":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"Commonwealth Labour",eyebrow:"Political party tradition · 1950s onward",infoboxKicker:"British social-democratic party",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 -->Commonwealth Labour is the accepted name for the post-defeat Labour tradition that reconciles social provision and strategic public industry with government of a defended Commonwealth.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author directly requires later Labour government, makes Labour responsible for the failed independence experiment and the limited Kuwait war aim, and uses Healey's near-socialist turn to prepare Thatcher's alternative.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes a later Labour government, greater Labour willingness to dismantle imperial control, Labour's grant of the failed West African independence, Labour restraint short of conquering Iraq, Healey's succession after Wilson's Kuwait victory, a near-socialist policy turn, and Thatcher's later displacement of that order.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Commonwealth Labour name, leadership sequence through Bevan, Gaitskell, Wilson, Healey and Martin Calder, detailed programme, exact elections, producer-state crisis, and later opposition and government roles are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Formal naming date, complete platforms, factions, organization, and membership remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins after defeat",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly requires Labour to return to government after the post-defeat Conservative settlement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Reconstruction Labour's 1947 entry into office and role in shipping, health, housing, arsenal, and Commonwealth-route institutions are accepted party history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The point when accommodation becomes a named current remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"bevan",title:"The Bevan inheritance",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p><a href='aneurin-bevan.html'>Aneurin Bevan's</a> accepted party inheritance includes health security, housing, public provision, arsenal capacity, and merchant shipping.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Commonwealth Labour's later interpretation of his democratic and administrative distrust is compatible political history.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"gaitskell",title:"Gaitskell's consolidation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p><a href='hugh-gaitskell.html'>Hugh Gaitskell's</a> 1960–1963 government and linkage of welfare, exports, productivity, accountable administration, and the Commonwealth system are accepted chronology and party interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Factional programme and policy texts remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"wilson",title:"Wilson's modernization",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that <a href='harold-wilson.html'>Harold Wilson</a> wins the Kuwait conflict.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His 1963–1964 and 1969–1976 ministries, Liberal-supported minority, small majority, 1975 election, and scientific, regional, computing, nuclear, and educational programme are accepted chronology and development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"program",title:"The Commonwealth Labour program",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly characterizes Labour as generally more willing to dismantle imperial control than its opponents.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Its accepted reconciliation of that tendency with the Crown, defended Commonwealth, strategic public industry, defense, modernization, housing, social security, and a social floor is accepted party synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Precise positions vary by ministry and remain open where not separately fixed.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"contradiction",title:"Imperial contradiction",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly makes Labour responsible for granting the failed West African independence and establishes that the resulting German-aligned revolutionary outcome discredits unrestricted independence and permanently damages the party.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Commonwealth Labour's continued administration of unequal protectorates, associated states, and bases is accepted political development; the party-level moral judgment remains analysis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"healey",title:"Healey and the producer state",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes <a href='denis-healey.html'>Healey's</a> succession after Wilson's Kuwait victory, Labour restraint short of conquering Iraq, and a later policy turn close enough to socialism to trouble the public.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1976 date, strategic public corporations, modernization, housing and social programme, soft budgets, controls, and 1978–1980 clearing crisis are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"opposition",title:"Opposition after 1980",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-l9s3k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T12:30:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly requires Thatcherism to emerge as the governing alternative by the early 1980s.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Labour's 1980 defeat, Martin Calder's 1988 leadership, acceptance of mixed ownership and defense, commercialization and audited contracts, and later return to government are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Organizational evolution remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"aneurin-bevan.html",kicker:"Reconstruction founder",label:"Aneurin Bevan"},{href:"harold-wilson.html",kicker:"Victory premier",label:"Harold Wilson"},{href:"denis-healey.html",kicker:"Producer-state premier",label:"Denis Healey"},{href:"national-liberal-constitutional-union.html",kicker:"Principal rival",label:"National Liberal–Constitutional Union"}],
    facts:[["Accepted precursor","Reconstruction Labour <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted constitutional position","Crown-in-Parliament <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted strategic system","British Commonwealth <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted economic position","Strategic public corporations and mixed economy <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted major leaders","Bevan, Gaitskell, Wilson, Healey <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted 1975 result","344 seats <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Established Healey turn","Near-socialist policy departure <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Accepted later leader","Martin Calder <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"]]
  }),

  "ordered-particularism":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"Ordered Particularism",eyebrow:"Commonwealth constitutional doctrine · 1960s onward",infoboxKicker:"Imperial constitutional doctrine",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 -->British Africa develops a permanent particularist order presented as unequal but dignified, treating different peoples and territories through different institutions while strategic necessity displaces metropolitan moral preference.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> <strong>Ordered Particularism</strong> and the maxim “One Crown peace; many forms of government” are accepted names for the Powellite doctrine built around that order.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 -->Permanent particularism, unequal but dignified treatment, different constitutional forms for different African societies, local legal administration, political ceilings, a civilizing mission without a fixed endpoint, Powell's advocacy, and continuing Crown association after the independence crisis are directly established.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The doctrine's name, Powellite codification, Two Falsehoods, maxim, legal minimum, enumerated forms and qualifications, Accra Clause, and later institutional influence are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Codifying statute, canonical text, first official use, and precise territorial adoption remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author establishes that British Africa cannot be governed through one metropolitan model and that practical local administration increasingly outranks liberal and Labour scruple.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Connecting this doctrine to the Breaking of the Raj and to improvised protectorates, leases, associated states, defended federations, and local compacts is accepted imperial synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The sequence of doctrinal formation remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"falsehoods",title:"Accepted Two Falsehoods",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly assigns Powell a role in negotiating morally with white African arguments and defending different constitutional treatment.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> His Two Falsehoods—one political endpoint for all peoples, and abstract rights unsupported by force, money, or consent—are accepted doctrinal formulation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Wording, venue, date, and publication remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"minimum",title:"Accepted common minimum",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author's order is explicitly presented as dignified despite inequality and is defended by comparison with German rule.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> A common minimum of life, property, worship, family, courts, and freedom from enslavement, guaranteed by Crown sovereignty or binding association, is accepted doctrine.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its legal enforceability and instruments remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"forms",title:"Forms of government",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes permanent particularism and rejects the assumption that every African society is immediately suited to the same democratic form.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Customary kingdoms, tribal federations, municipalities, protectorates, settler assemblies, industrial boards, professional bodies, and territorial legislatures are accepted examples.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their legal categories and territorial distribution remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"qualifications",title:"Qualifications and political ceilings",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>Unequal legal treatment, long-term political ceilings, and the claim that different populations require different administration are direct authorial premises.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Literacy, property, taxation, service, profession, locality, and recognized community as nominally non-racial qualifications are accepted mechanisms.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Their reproduction of inherited racial power is compatible analysis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact franchises remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"accra",title:"Accra settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that the failed independence ends unrestricted sovereignty for an extended period and changes later demands toward Crown, imperial, or dominion association.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Ghana, the 1963 chronology, and the <a href='accra-clause.html'>Accra Clause</a>'s Crown, defense-association, and multilateral-guarantee mechanisms are accepted constitutional development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"practice",title:"Accepted applications",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>Nigeria's Crown federation, frontier territories, East African common services, southern African security autonomy, Gulf protected states, and varied British relationships in India are accepted applications of the doctrine.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Separate constitutional instruments in place of one imperial citizenship are compatible institutional synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Formal adoption and chronology remain open by territory.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"criticism",title:"Reception and legacy",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-b8r6k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T22:00:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes a morally compromised order justified by strategic necessity and by the claim that German rule would be worse.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Supporters' language of diversity and enforceable duty, critics' description of a lawful permanent empire, and survival in Crown Conservatism and parts of the NLCU are accepted reception and legacy.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Party texts and later doctrinal boundaries remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"enoch-powell.html",kicker:"Principal theorist",label:"Enoch Powell"},{href:"british-africa.html",kicker:"Principal application",label:"British Africa"},{href:"commonwealth.html",kicker:"Constitutional system",label:"British Commonwealth"},{href:"national-liberal-movement.html",kicker:"Later inheritance",label:"National Liberal Movement"}],
    facts:[["Established associated figure","Enoch Powell"],["Accepted maxim","One Crown peace; many forms of government"],["Proposed common floor","Life, property, worship, family, courts, freedom from enslavement"],["Established political endpoint","No universal fixed form"],["Accepted franchise","Graduated and qualified"],["Accepted treaty mechanism","Accra Clause"],["Established principal arena","British Africa"],["Established criticism","Permanent unequal political ceilings"]]
  }),

  "national-liberal-movement":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"National Liberal movement",eyebrow:"British political movement · 1960s–1980",infoboxKicker:"Market-Commonwealth political tendency",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 -->The National Liberal movement is the accepted cross-party vehicle joining competitive enterprise, household property, strong defense, Crown and Commonwealth, and preservation of strategic national capacity.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its organization around Margaret Thatcher translates the author's direct premise that she appears conservative in some manners while introducing an economically liberal programme without precedent in the setting.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes Thatcher's liberal-economic shock, real but limited improvement, pragmatic factory reownership, and the public's willingness to try her alternative after Healey.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The National Liberal name, Thatcher leadership of a cross-party movement, constituency, market-Commonwealth formula, strategic-commercial distinction, Liberal and Conservative sources, selective Powellite inheritance, and consolidation into the NLCU before 1980 are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Founding date, precursor clubs, organization, and membership remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"origins",title:"Origins in the post-defeat settlement",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The Contract Commonwealth's combination of markets, public guarantees, strategic corporations, and negotiated production, followed by 1960s criticism of permanent producer shelter, is accepted political-economic development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Dates, organizations, and named early advocates remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"thatcher",title:"Thatcher's break",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly defines <a href='margaret-thatcher.html'>Thatcher</a> as conservative in some manners but economically liberal, without a prior setting precedent.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Her Conservative beginning, movement toward Liberal enterprise, and insistence that hard budgets need not abandon the fleet, Crown, Commonwealth, nuclear power, or national survival capacity are accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"constituency",title:"Social constituency",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The movement's appeal to small businesses, shopkeepers, professionals, technical entrepreneurs, younger managers, homeowners, anti-corporatist Conservatives, and economically liberal Commonwealth nationalists is accepted electoral sociology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Membership, regional strength, and polling remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"doctrine",title:"The National Liberal formula",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The compact formula of strong defense, Crown and Commonwealth, a freer internal economy, strategic national capacity, and hostility to socialist or corporatist administration is accepted party synthesis.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> It gives institutional form to the author's direct requirement that Thatcher improve the economy in some respects without becoming an infallible free-market figure.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"capacity",title:"Strategic capacity and commercial discipline",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes temporary state acquisition and lease-to-buy transfer of failed de facto public factories as Thatcher's pragmatic concession.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The broader distinction between collectively secured nuclear power, grid, rail, telecommunications, dockyards, arsenals, launch and reserve assets, and commercially disciplined non-strategic firms is accepted programme detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"currents",title:"Liberals, Conservatives, and Powellites",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The allocation of legalism and civil economy to Liberals, organization and Crown loyalty to Conservative defectors, and sovereignty and defense to some Powellites is accepted coalition interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The movement's rejection of Powell's cultural severity and of Ordered Particularism as a complete domestic philosophy is compatible differentiation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"crisis",title:"The Healey crisis",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that Healey's policies near socialism trouble the public enough to create willingness to try Thatcher's alternative.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1978–1980 clearing and contracts crisis and the National Liberal distinction between a necessary arsenal and a whole protected economy are accepted causal development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"union",title:"Consolidation into the NLCU",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-n3l7k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:55:00-06:00 --><p>The movement's consolidation before the 1980 election as the organizing core of the <a href='national-liberal-constitutional-union.html'>National Liberal–Constitutional Union</a> is accepted political chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its federated form and presentation of Thatcher as a candidate for government are accepted institutional detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Founding instrument remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"margaret-thatcher.html",kicker:"Principal leader",label:"Margaret Thatcher"},{href:"national-liberal-constitutional-union.html",kicker:"Electoral vehicle",label:"National Liberal–Constitutional Union"},{href:"ordered-particularism.html",kicker:"Partial inheritance",label:"Ordered Particularism"},{href:"commonwealth-labour.html",kicker:"Principal rival",label:"Commonwealth Labour"}],
    facts:[["Accepted period","1960s–1980"],["Accepted principal leader","Margaret Thatcher"],["Accepted political sources","Liberals, Conservatives, constitutional market liberals, some Powellites"],["Accepted social base","Small firms, professionals, managers, homeowners"],["Established economic departure","Liberal reform with pragmatic safeguards"],["Accepted constitutional principle","Crown and Commonwealth"],["Established reownership device","Factory lease-to-buy"],["Accepted successor vehicle","National Liberal–Constitutional Union"]]
  }),

  "national-liberal-constitutional-union":laterBritishArticle({
    title:"National Liberal–Constitutional Union",eyebrow:"British political party · formed before 1980",infoboxKicker:"British governing union",infoboxTitle:"National Liberal–Constitutional Union",
    lead:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4a writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 -->The National Liberal–Constitutional Union, commonly the NLCU, is the accepted governing union assembled around Margaret Thatcher before the 1980 election from National Liberals, Liberals, defecting Conservatives, constitutional market liberals, and some Powellites.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Its market discipline with protected strategic capacity gives party form to the author's direct premise of liberal-economic reform tempered by pragmatic factory reownership.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    canon:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4b writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 -->The author directly establishes Thatcher's unprecedented liberal economics, lease-to-buy factory safeguard, limited improvement, Falklands overconfidence and adaptation, hard-won 1986 victory, and voluntary exhausted retirement.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The NLCU name and constituents, Thatcher leadership, 1980 total of 337 seats, detailed mixed-economy programme, named Reownership Act, 1985 totals, 1987 succession, and Ashcombe continuation are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Formal founding date, constitution, organization, membership, faction strength, and exact support terms remain open.<!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->",
    sections:[
      {id:"formation",title:"Formation",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4c writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The National Liberal movement's organization before 1980 into a governing vehicle joining Liberals, defecting Conservatives, constitutional market liberals, and some Powellites is accepted political development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Timing and mechanics of local association, candidate, and parliamentary transfers remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"constituents",title:"Constituent currents",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4d writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The combination of National Liberals, Liberals, defecting Conservatives, constitutional market liberals, and some Powellites, together with the assigned contribution of each current, is accepted coalition reconstruction.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Relative faction strength remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"ideology",title:"Ideology",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4e writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes liberal-economic reform, actual but limited improvement, and a pragmatic lease-to-buy concession for failed factories.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The NLCU platform of Crown, Commonwealth, strategic public or controlled capacity, hard accounts, competition, partial sale, and employee ownership is accepted institutional formulation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Precise nuclear, monetary, housing, and organizational policy remains open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"election",title:"Victory in 1980",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4f writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The NLCU's 1980 victory with 337 seats on 43 percent against Commonwealth Labour's 274 on 38 percent is accepted electoral chronology.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> The author directly establishes Thatcher's arrival as a shock to a system without precedent for her economic liberalism.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> Detailed mandate and voting geography remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"government",title:"Government and reownership",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4g writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes temporary state acquisition of failed de facto public factories and lease-to-buy transfer to competent private operators, often preserving local employment after an interruption.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The Industrial Reconstitution and Reownership Act 1981, Industrial Custodianship Corporation, Preserve–Lease–Transfer formula, asset division, and strategic-retention rules are accepted institutional detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"war",title:"Falklands government",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4h writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes Thatcher's initial military overconfidence, a damaging early failure, public scrutiny during a long televised war, adaptation, and acceptance of responsibility together with credit for the hard-won victory.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The 1984 invasion, Reownership contribution, Second Torch diagnosis, and No Third Torch formulation are accepted chronology and interpretation.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"runway",title:"The Runway Election",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4j writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes that Thatcher's second wartime election is constitutionally required rather than evidence that she wants another full term.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The mid-1985 date, 317 NLCU members, support from 18 Crown-and-Commonwealth Conservatives, Labour's 289, and the interpretation as continuation under scrutiny are accepted detail.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup> Exact voting date and support agreement remain open.</p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"},
      {id:"succession",title:"Thatcher succession",html:"<!-- altwwii-writer-block:start id=wb-g-20260816-c6v4k writer=g kind=revision created=2026-08-16T23:59:00-06:00 --><p>The author directly establishes an exhausted Thatcher seeking an early postwar dissolution, declining to run, and retiring voluntarily after the 1986 conclusion.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup> The late-1987 timing, <a href='1987-nlcu-leadership-election.html'>leadership election</a>, choice of <a href='david-ashcombe.html'>David Ashcombe</a> over Julian Rooke and Matthew Halden, and Responsible Independence programme are accepted development.<sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup></p><!-- altwwii-writer-block:end -->"}
    ],
    related:[{href:"national-liberal-movement.html",kicker:"Precursor current",label:"National Liberal Movement"},{href:"margaret-thatcher.html",kicker:"Founding premier",label:"Margaret Thatcher"},{href:"1987-nlcu-leadership-election.html",kicker:"Leadership succession",label:"1987 NLCU Leadership Election"},{href:"david-ashcombe.html",kicker:"Successor premier",label:"David Ashcombe"}],
    facts:[["Accepted abbreviation","NLCU <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted principal founder","Margaret Thatcher <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted constituents","National Liberals, Liberals, Conservative defectors, constitutional market liberals, some Powellites <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted 1980 result","337 seats <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted 1985 result","317 seats <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Accepted allied support","18 Crown-and-Commonwealth Conservatives <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"],["Established economic device","Factory lease-to-buy <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='a'>[a]</sup>"],["Accepted leader from late 1987","David Ashcombe <sup class='canon-note' data-provenance='e'>[e]</sup>"]]
  })
});

const addLaterBritishRelated = (slug,item) => {
  const related = window.deepArticles[slug]?.related;
  if (related && !related.some(entry=>entry.href===item.href)) related.unshift(item);
};

